          %p#BieberbachTheorem
            This transparent description of crystallographic groups is essentially the content of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bieberbach%27s+first+theorem"} Bieberbach's first theorem
            (following
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bieberbach1910"} Bieberbach 1910
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Farkas81"} Farkas 1981, Thm. 14
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Charlap86"} Charlap 1986, Thm. I 3.1
            %a{:href => "#Engel86"} Engel 1986, Thm. 3.1
            for modern accounts and find a concise statement in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Tolcachier19"} Tolcachier 19, Thm. 2.3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FreedMoore13"} Freed &amp; Moore 2013, (0.2)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Farkas81"} Farkas 81, Sec. 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Iso
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(E)
            such that the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+group"} compact
          %p
            If the crystallographic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            on the left of
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CrystallographicInclusionOfShortExactSequences"} (1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+exact+sequence"} splits
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⋉
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \simeq G \ltimes N
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semidirect+product"} semidirect product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+group"} point group
            with the translational lattice, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong symmorphic space group
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugacy+class"} conjugacy class
            of a crystallographic group (under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjugation"} conjugation
            by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/O%28n%29"} O(n)
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bravais+lattice"} Bravais lattice
            of the corresponding crystal lattice.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#Classification Classification
          %p
            In 2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensions"} dimensions
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wallpaper+groups"} wallpaper groups
          %p
            In 3 dimensions, there are 219 distinct types, or 230 if chiral copies are considered distinct. The classification of space groups has been carried out up to dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=6
          %p
            On the classification of symmorphic space groups see also
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/a/77882/381"} MO:a/77882
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ChuprunovKuntsevich88"} Chuprunov-Kuntsevich 88
          %blockquote
            %p
              Let us make a brief survey of the main achievements in the
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a{:href => "#BrownBulowNeubuserWondratschekZassenhaus78"} “Crystallographic groups of four- dimensional space” by Brown et al.
              [15]. These groups were derived on the base of the nine maximal arithmetic crystal classes, derived by Dade in 1965, which allowed one to determine all of the 710 four-dimensional arithmetic classes and to calculate the normalizers of finite groups of the unimodular 4 x 4 matrixes needed for the Zassenhaus algorithm. The monograph [15] is of interest not only by having a complete description of all classes of the four-dimensional crystallographic groups but also by taking a deeper approach to the system of classification of the n-dimensional crystallographic groups, as well as by giving characteristic properties of the four-dimensional crystallographic groups in comparison with that in lower dimensions. One of these properties is enantiomorphizm exhibited not only by the space group types but also by Bravais types of lattices, arithmetic classes and geometric classes. For the first time this phenomenon was found by Shtogrin [18].
            %p The n-dimensional mathematical crystallography is still in progess. Ryshkov [19] determined all maximal arithmetic crystal classes of five-dimensional Euclidean space. Some categories of five- and six-dimensional “small” groups isomorphic to the three-dimensional groups of symmetry, anti- symmetry, two-fold antisymmetry, p- and p’-symmetry were derived by Palistrant [20]. Some aspects of the mathematical theory applied to the n-dimensional crystallography were considered [15, 21].
          %h3#CompactFlatOrbifolds Compact flat orbifolds
          #InducedPointGroupActionOnTorus.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (induced point group action on torus)
            %p
              The assumption that the crystallographic translation group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \subset S
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi S
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    1 \to N \longrightarrow S \longrightarrow G \to 1
            %p
              implies that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+group"} point group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi N
